  "image_name": "754f2b275b57e1aaa03c830b36c61e34.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two farming systems and links them to soil condition. At the top, industrial agriculture is shown as simplified monocropping with crop residue removal, bare soil between seasons, heavy ploughing, and strong dependence on mineral fertilizers and pesticides. Large tractors and notes about heavy vehicles, regular tyres, and driving on wet soils emphasize compaction. The resulting soil profile on the right appears degraded: roots are sparse and shallow, the soil looks compact and biologically poor, and the crop aboveground seems less vigorous.\n\nIn contrast, the lower half presents conservation agriculture as a more diversified system. It combines crop rotation, intercropping, agroforestry, cover cropping, residue return, and reduced tillage. Inputs are broader and more biologically oriented, including compost, biochar, crop residues, biologics, and integrated soil fertility and pest management, while pesticides and mineral fertilizers appear less central. Soil management also aims to reduce compaction through lighter tractors, track systems, and avoiding work on wet ground. The corresponding soil profile looks intact, with denser roots, more organic matter and soil organisms, and a healthier plant. Overall, the image argues that diversified, lower-disturbance practices support healthier, less degraded soils than intensive conventional management.",
